  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Two-banded Cellophane-cuckoo Bee (Epeolus bifasciatus) is a small parasitic bee that belongs to the Apidae family, not a plant as might be suggested by the prompt. This cuckoo bee is a cleptoparasite that lays its eggs in the nests of other bee species, particularly sweat bees.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Two Abdominal Bands",
      "description": "Distinctive pale yellow or white bands on the second and third abdominal segments",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Approximately 8-10mm in length, making it one of the smaller bee species in New England",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Sparse Body Hair",
      "description": "Noticeably less hairy than host bee species, an adaptation to cuckoo bee lifestyle",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Two-banded Cellophane-cuckoo Bees are found in open woodlands, gardens, and meadows throughout New England where their host bee species are present.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike typical bees, cuckoo bees like Epeolus bifasciatus do not collect pollen or produce honey; instead, they are brood parasites that sneak into the nests of other bees to lay their eggs, relying on host bees to provision their larvae.",
